[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Visitor Pattern for Operations on Object Structures"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Visitor Pattern"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Visitor Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that lets you separate algorithms from the objects on which they operate. This pattern is particularly useful when you need to perform diverse and unrelated operations on a collection of heterogeneous objects (an object structure) without polluting their classes with that operational logic."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Problem Statement and Motivation"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Imagine you have a complex object structure representing a document model, containing paragraphs, images, and tables. You need to implement features like exporting to PDF, exporting to HTML, and running spell checks. If you add these methods directly to the element classes, the codebase quickly becomes bloated and violates the Single Responsibility Principle. Furthermore, adding a new operation would require modifying every single element class."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Architecture"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Visitor Interface: Declares a visit method for each concrete element type in the object structure."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concrete Visitors: Implement specific operations to be performed on the object structure, storing necessary state for that operation."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Element Interface: Declares an accept method that takes a visitor as an argument."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concrete Elements: Implement the accept method by calling the appropriate visit method on the visitor object."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Python Implementation Example"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Visitor(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def visit_paragraph(self, paragraph):
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    def visit_image(self, image):
        pass

class Element(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def accept(self, visitor):
        pass

class Paragraph(Element):
    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text

    def accept(self, visitor):
        visitor.visit_paragraph(self)

class Image(Element):
    def __init__(self, url):
        self.url = url

    def accept(self, visitor):
        visitor.visit_image(self)

class HTMLExportVisitor(Visitor):
    def visit_paragraph(self, paragraph):
        print(f"<p>{paragraph.text}</p>")

    def visit_image(self, image):
        print(f"<img src=\"{image.url}\" />")

document = [Paragraph("Hello World"), Image("logo.png")]
html_visitor = HTMLExportVisitor()

for element in document:
    element.accept(html_visitor)"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of the Visitor Pattern"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open/Closed Principle: You can introduce a new behavior to existing object structures without modifying their classes."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Responsibility Principle: Related behaviors are gathered in one single class (the visitor) rather than being scattered across many element classes."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Visitors can accumulate state as they traverse through a complex object structure."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Drawbacks and Considerations"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary drawback of the Visitor Pattern is the difficulty of adding new Concrete Element classes. Every time a new element type is added, all visitor implementations must be updated to include a corresponding visit method. Additionally, it can break encapsulation if element classes must expose internal state to allow visitors to perform their operations."}]
